--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REASONING TRACE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. FULL PROJECT READ: Agent read all key files — main.py, api.py, all endpoint
     modules, signals.py (pipeline code), specialists.py, tracer.py, models —
     to build a complete mental model of the system before proposing any changes.

  2. API GAP ANALYSIS: Compared what the frontend mobile app was already using
     vs what a web dashboard would need to show a complete demo to judges.
     Found that 3 types of data had NO endpoint: dashboard counters, agent states,
     and a global cross-incident log feed.

  3. DASHBOARD STATS DESIGN: Decided to query 4 tables in a single endpoint:
     - Signal table → total_signals (COUNT)
     - Incident table → active_crisis_sectors (COUNT with status filter)
     - ReasoningLog table → total_agent_decisions (COUNT)
     - Resource table → allocated_ambulances + allocated_rescue_crews
       (sum of total_count - available_count per resource type)

  4. AGENT WORKFORCE DESIGN: Decided to infer live agent state from the
     reasoning_logs table. For each of the 8 agents, fetch their most recent log.
     If the log is < 15 seconds old → agent is PROCESSING. Otherwise → IDLE.
     This approach requires no schema changes and uses existing data.
     The 15-second cutoff was chosen to match the approximate time between
     sequential agent steps in the pipeline.

  5. GLOBAL TIMELINE DESIGN: Simple query — SELECT from reasoning_logs
     ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 15. Returns a cross-incident view of the
     most recent 15 agent actions. Ideal for judge-facing dashboards.

  6. FILE CREATION: Created dashboard.py with all 3 endpoints using the same
     async SQLAlchemy session pattern as the other endpoint files.

  7. ROUTER REGISTRATION: Updated api.py to import and register the new dashboard
     router under the /dashboard prefix with "Dashboard" OpenAPI tag.

  8. TEST EXECUTION: Wrote test_dashboard_apis.py using FastAPI TestClient with
     context manager (required for proper startup event handling with async DB).
     First run failed due to system Python not having sqlmodel installed.
     Fixed: Used venv Python → .\venv\Scripts\python.exe test_dashboard_apis.py

  9. TEST RESULT — ALL 3 ENDPOINTS PASSED HTTP 200:
     - /dashboard/stats          → 200 OK ✅
     - /dashboard/agent-workforce → 200 OK ✅
     - /dashboard/global-timeline → 200 OK ✅

     Script crashed AFTER successful API calls on the print() statement due to
     Windows PowerShell cp1252 encoding rejecting emoji characters (📢, 🔍)
     stored in the reasoning_logs from the AI agents. This is a terminal display
     issue, NOT an API failure. All 3 APIs confirmed working.

  10. ADDITIONAL GAP ANALYSIS: After implementing the 3 dashboard APIs, performed
      a deeper analysis for judge-demo completeness. Found 3 remaining gaps:

      GAP 1 — Actions Endpoint (MISSING):
        The actions table has tactical operations from the Planning Agent
        (DISPATCH_RESCUE_TEAM, REROUTE_TRAFFIC, etc.) but no GET endpoint exists
        to retrieve them per incident. Without this, judges cannot see what
        physical actions the system decided to take.
        Needed: GET /api/v1/incidents/{id}/actions

      GAP 2 — Resources Endpoint (MISSING):
        The resources table tracks the current ambulance/rescue_crew pool but
        has no GET endpoint. Dashboard cannot show the "14 ambulances allocated"
        type of visual.
        Needed: GET /api/v1/resources

      GAP 3 — Pipeline Progress Gap (ARCHITECTURAL):
        When a signal is submitted, the pipeline runs in the background.
        The WebSocket broadcasts early agent logs (Signal → Detection → Severity)
        to the "triage" channel, but the frontend LiveLogStream connects to a
        specific incidentId WebSocket channel — which doesn't exist yet because
        the incident is only created AFTER step 3 completes (~40-90 seconds later).
        Result: All early agent logs are LOST from the frontend's perspective.
        The blank screen at the start is caused by two separate issues:
          (a) No incident in DB yet (pipeline running time — inherent delay)
          (b) Frontend can't subscribe to "triage" channel without knowing signal_id
        Fix approach: Return signal_id from POST /signals → frontend immediately
        connects to ws/.../triage_{signal_id} → early logs stream live → when
        incident is confirmed, switch to ws/.../incidentId.

